From f8a798da744c72226800f90566f9e35e8bccab4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 07:30:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 072/119] scene-referred S2: sun shafts that carry the sun

Fourth item of Stage S2. Sun shafts are the clearest case in the engine of an effect built
entirely around a display-referred assumption: a mask that clips to a silhouette, a colour whose
magnitude is thrown away, and a composite that blends against the remaining headroom of a
finished picture. Under a pre-exposure they are the one thing in the frame that does not follow
the aperture. D13 forbids turning them off, so they are rebuilt in scene units instead.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s2-sun-shafts.md, approach A. Research:
scene-notes/research/s2-sun-shafts.md.

- The working set goes fp16 under the switch. $DisplayTarget 1/2a, 1/4a, 1/8 and their two Temp
  siblings are re-formatted through the same CTexture::ReformatRenderTarget() path S0 built for
  the HDR satellites, with the same all-or-none rule - they are ping-ponged AND addressable by
  name from content-side shader scripts as $backbufferscaled_d2 / _d4 / _d8, so a mixed set
  would be a silent precision cliff content could stumble into. nLDRPFormat is untouched, so
  with the switch off SSAO's albedo downsample, the snow velocity passes and the legacy
  PostEffects paths that share these targets are bit-for-bit stock.

  MEMORY COST of the fp16 working set: R10G10B10A2 is 4 B/px, RGBA16F is 8. For the whole set of
  five, about +5 MiB at 1080p and +21 MiB at 4K, PER GRAPHICS PIPELINE - Sandbox pays it again
  for every extra viewport. The shaft pair alone is +4 MiB / +17 MiB of that. Bandwidth is about
  +40 MB/frame at 1080p and +158 MB/frame at 4K across the three shaft passes, i.e. 0.2-2.4 % of
  a typical discrete GPU. The fp16 working set is cheap; it was the format's content
  compatibility that made it a decision. Logged once per flip like the HDR satellites.

  This is the one place the implementation follows the task and the decision note rather than the
  research note, which preferred a dedicated fp16 pair so the shared UNORM targets never move.
  Re-formatting the shared set is safe here because it only ever happens under the switch, and it
  keeps one mechanism instead of two.

- sunColor.Normalize() is skipped on the scene-referred path. It keeps only the sun's hue as a
  unit vector, so a white sun is (0.577, 0.577, 0.577) at every time of day and shaft brightness
  is the amount slider and nothing else. That is a workaround for the UNORM target, not a
  rendering decision. The scene-referred path uses the sun's real radiance times the latched
  pre-exposure - E3DPARAM_SUN_COLOR is the UNEXPOSED sun, the pre-exposure lives one level down
  in CRenderView::GetSunLightColor(), which this call does not go through - so the shafts now
  follow T-stop, ND and time of day continuously. The SunShafts_* parameters keep their meaning
  exactly: the amount is the fraction of the sun the shafts carry. Its useful VALUE changes, from
  a display fraction to a scattering coefficient of order 0.01-0.05; the calibration is written
  up in docs/SceneReferredCalibration.md section 3.

- The composite moves before the tone curve. HDRFinalPass now carries BOTH composites, selected
  by %_RT_SAMPLE0 - the scene-referred permutation of this pass, claimed here and reused by S4
  for the output transform, which is exactly why S1 pinned the exposure with a constant instead
  of spending this bit. The stock composite is textually untouched and simply unreachable when
  SAMPLE0 is set. The new one adds the shafts into the scene sample BEFORE FilmMapping, and adds
  them plainly: the stock (1 - oSample) headroom term has no meaning in scene-referred space,
  where there is no "1.0 white" to have headroom against, and scattering is physically additive
  anyway. The visible consequence, which D13 says to let show, is that shafts are no longer
  suppressed over bright regions.

- GetDownscaledTargetsIndex() is pinned to half resolution under the switch. At eRQ_Medium and
  below the stock code selects index 1, whose HDR source target is never filled when bloom and
  auto-exposure are both off, because the quarter-res downsample does not list the shafts stage
  among its triggers. That is a stock bug; fixing it would change the stock path at medium
  quality, so the scene-referred path steps around it instead.

Known breakage carried forward, not hidden: the blur's chained gain (8 taps of weights summing
to 4.5, divided by 8 and multiplied by 2, twice) is an amplification of 1.2656x that UNORM used
to clip away and fp16 no longer does, and the mask (linearDepth * 1.32 * (R+G+B)) stops being a
silhouette and becomes a real luminance image - shafts now track what is actually bright, which
is the desired behaviour and also the biggest visual change. Neither is normalised here; both are
now explicit, documented gains to be calibrated against the picture. Shafts also have no sky
test, so bright geometry near the sun's screen position still makes rays, and more strongly than
before. And the composite now lands before the temporal resolve, so a moving camera must be
checked for highlight crawl before this is signed off.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): CryRenderD3D11.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A72MFkdSczncE7au9UVnmu
